# Header includes vs forward declares

In `.h`, forward-declare types that appear only as pointers, references, or `TObjectPtr`. Include the full header in the `.cpp` that calls members or needs a complete type.

```cpp
// ✅ header
class UPawnUIComponent;
UPawnUIComponent* GetPawnUIComponent() const;

// ✅ cpp
#include "Components/UI/PawnUIComponent.h"
```

```cpp
// ❌ header — pulls Foo.h into every dependent TU
#include "Foo.h"
class UBar { Foo* Foo; };
```

Must include in the header when the type is a base class, a by-value member, or a template argument that needs a complete type (e.g. `TWeakInterfacePtr<IPawnUIInterface>`).

Do not include a UCLASS header in a public `.h` only to mention a pointer. Extra includes in Public headers inflate incremental compile cost and cause include cycles with `*.generated.h`.
